(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608170300-cljs-runner-completeness-gate")
  (:adr/title "Moving a gate to ClojureScript needs a fourth condition: that the runner runs the suite. Five measured corrections took the detector from 779 findings to 23")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-17")
  (:source/dataset "adr")
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/superseded-by "")
  (:adr/context
  "ADR-2608170200 moved three `:jvm-test` fleet gates to `:nbb-test` on three measured
conditions, and named the trap it had walked into: `kotoba-lang/columnar` shipped three
test namespaces and two ClojureScript runners, each requiring one of the three. The JVM
suite ran 36 tests / 117 assertions; nbb ran 10 / 54. With `group/group` altered to drop
the last group from every grouped result, the partial runner printed
`Ran 10 tests ... 0 failures` and exited 0.

The `:nbb-test` gate's own floor only requires that SOME `Ran N tests` line appear, which
a one-third runner satisfies. Condition 1 of that ADR -- identical counts under both
runtimes -- catches this, but only while a human is running both sides and comparing. The
whole point of the flip is that `clojure -M:test` stops running, and with it the second
number. After the flip there is nothing left to compare against.

So the condition that actually has to hold is not 'the two numbers matched on the day we
flipped'. It is 'the ClojureScript runner runs the suite', continuously, and nothing in
the workspace measured that.")

  (:adr/decision
  "1. **`scripts/verify-cljs-runner-completeness.cljs`**, registered as the 12th
     orgs-detector (`:verify-cljs-runner-completeness`, 24h, `:writes :none`). It compares
     each project's ClojureScript runners against the `deftest`-bearing portable files in
     the subtree they sit in.

  2. **A fourth admission condition** at the head of `gates.edn` `:repos`: a `:jvm-test`
     gate may become `:nbb-test` only if this detector reports the repo complete.

  3. **Classification is by CONTENT, never by filename.** A runner is a non-test
     `.cljs`/`.cljc` file that calls `run-tests`/`run-all-tests`; a test namespace is a
     portable file containing `deftest`. This is what keeps
     `kotobase-storage/test/run.cljs` out of scope without an exemption list -- it is a
     hand-written async oracle for the Worker path, calls no `run-tests`, and prints no
     `Ran N tests` line at all. It is a different suite, not an incomplete port.

  4. **The unit is the project and the question is the UNION of its runners**, because
     whether a repo splits runners by subject or by platform is intent that cannot be read
     from source. What can be read, and what matters, is whether a namespace is run by no
     runner at all.")

  (:adr/body
  "## What it measures (2026-08-17, 4,163 of 4,184 registered checkouts, 34s)

201 repos are in scope -- they have a ClojureScript runner AND at least one portable test.
225 runners; 366 oracle scripts counted and excluded. **179 of 201 complete; 23 findings
over 22 repos.**

| kind | n | meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `:suite-uncovered` | 21 | no runner in the project names the namespace |
| `:runner-loaded-not-run` | 2 | required, but handed to no `run-tests`, and no `run-all-tests` |
| `:runner-runs-nothing` | 0 | `(run-tests)` with no target — runs its own namespace |
| `:runner-unreadable` | 0 | the honest refusal |

Both directions on the same repo, with the real defect and the real fix: columnar at
`6e417bc` reports 2 of 3 uncovered; at `5e165f0` it is complete. All four finding paths
were additionally shown red and green on real trees.

Exits: 0 in monitor mode, 1 with `--strict`, and **2** -- neither -- when west.yml has no
`path:` entries or its paths have no checkouts. Could-not-look must not share a value with
looked-and-clean.

## The most interesting catch is not columnar

`kotoba-lang/kotobase-peer` ships 17 `_test.cljc` and 2 `_test.cljs`. Its
`run-nbb-tests.cljs` walks `test/`, turns paths into namespaces, writes a static-require
entry file and runs nbb on it -- a good pattern, with its own zero floor (`no *_test.cljc
files found` -> exit 1). But it discovers `_test.cljc` only. Those two `.cljs` namespaces
run under shadow-cljs (`:ns-regexp \"-test$\"`) and never under nbb, while the runner's own
docstring calls itself *a genuine 3rd platform tier alongside JVM and self-hosted cljs*.

That is the same defect as columnar's arriving by a completely different route, and it is
why auto-discovery is not treated as a blanket pass: the literals a discovering runner
matches on are extracted and applied, rather than assumed exhaustive.

## Five measured corrections, 779 -> 23

Every one was found by opening the repo a finding named and comparing by hand. The first
version's 779 findings would have been, in the workspace's own vocabulary, theatre.

| # | wrong rule | what it did | n |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | test files are `*_test` | read 1,081 `test_*` files as RUNNERS | ~690 |
| 2 | judge each runner alone | called `gftdcojp/tia`'s five deliberate subsystem runners defects | ~29 |
| 3 | the repo is the unit | asked `etzhayyim/root`'s akashi runner to cover all 1,033 tests in that monorepo (`1074 of 1079`) | 1 huge |
| 4 | a runner must have `(ns ...)` | declared 35 ordinary nbb scripts unmeasurable; one of them (`cloud-itonami/loop-noren`) was complete | 35 |
| 5 | quoting is `'ns` | akashi writes `(quote ns)`, so its five genuinely-run namespaces looked unrun | 5 |
| 6 | a runner is a source file | `nekko`'s `test:async` is three `nbb -e \"(require '[ns])\"` invocations in **package.json**, invisible to a source-only audit | 3 |
| 7 | a test namespace needs a runner | a namespace ending in a bare top-level `(run-tests)` runs **itself** | (same 3) |

(6) and (7) came from `kotoba-lang/nekko`, reported as 16 of 17 uncovered. Three of
those sixteen were running the whole time. Corrected, it reports 13, and those
thirteen are genuinely JVM-only. Both corrections are the `_test$` error again in
new clothes: **looking in one place and concluding about all of them.**

## What the completion ratio does not say

`181 of 201 complete` is about the repos that HAVE a ClojureScript runner. Measured
2026-08-17, another **2,034** hold a `deftest` in a portable file and have no
`run-tests` call anywhere -- their portable suites run under `clojure -M:test` and
nowhere else (spot-checked: `cloud-itonami/action-gtin-registry`, `actor-crew`,
`actor-shukubo`). The real share of portable suites any ClojureScript host runs is
**201 of 2,235, about 9%**. Those 2,034 are counted and printed, never emitted as
findings: an absent runner is not an incomplete one and nothing there is fixed by
editing a require list. A `:jvm-test` gate in one of them cannot be flipped at all,
because admission condition 1 has no second number to compare against.

A first pass at that number said 860, from a shell probe that filtered test files by
NAME and dropped repos through a `head -1`. The name-filter error is the one this
script had already corrected internally; it recurred the moment the question was
asked outside it.

Measured naming conventions behind (1): of the portable files under a test directory,
5,690 are `*_test`, **1,081 are `test_*`**, 35 `*_tests`, 6 bare `test`/`tests`, 86 contain
`run`, and 270 are something else (`block_vectors`, `browser_harness`,
`apex_worker_smoke`). No filename rule survives that. `deftest` and `run-tests` are exact.

Correction (3) needed two boundaries, not one: nearest `deps.edn` ancestor was not enough,
because that monorepo's subprojects carry none, so akashi still collapsed to the root. The
subtree the runner SITS IN -- a `test/`/`tests/` component stripped back to its parent,
else its own directory -- is what scopes it correctly, and project membership is decided by
path containment rather than by comparing computed labels, so a test outside any `test/`
directory cannot land in a runner-less project and vanish.

Correction (4) is the mirror image of the failure this whole family of detectors exists to
prevent. **Refusing to answer a question you can answer is the same defect as answering
one you cannot, pointed the other way** -- and it is quieter, because a refusal looks
conscientious. The honest refusal is now `:runner-unreadable`, which needs the file read to
actually fail.

## What is NOT claimed

The 22 repos are reported, not repaired. Each needs its runner's require list and
`run-tests` call brought up to its suite -- the same two-line change columnar's fix made --
and then the suite has to be run to confirm it still passes, which is per-repo work.

This does not measure whether a covered test PASSES under ClojureScript, only that it is
run. A `.cljc` test whose body is `#?(:clj ...)` throughout is reported as uncovered when
absent from the runner, because the reported fact -- the ClojureScript run covers less than
the JVM run -- is true either way; why it is true is the repo owner's call.")

  (:adr/consequences
  "- `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn` gains a 12th detector. First run records a baseline of
  23; after that only NEW and RESOLVED surface, and a NEW one means a runner just stopped
  covering its suite -- precisely the moment coverage silently drops.
- `gates.edn` gains a fourth admission condition for `:jvm-test` -> `:nbb-test`. 22 of the
  201 repos with a ClojureScript runner do not currently pass it, so the condition has
  teeth before anyone flips the next gate.
- The three gates already flipped (kagami, columnar, org-ietf-csv) all pass condition 4,
  checked after the fact.
- The detector's own calibration history is recorded above rather than smoothed away,
  because a detector's first number is the least trustworthy thing about it, and five of
  five corrections here made it quieter, not louder.")

  (:adr/evidence
  (vector "scripts/verify-cljs-runner-completeness.cljs"
   "manifest/orgs-detectors.edn"
